---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "glo protein powder",
      "glo protein powder gabbi",
      "ours protein powder",
      "gabbi tuft protein powder",
      "glo by gabbi protein",
      "glo protein reviews"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Clean Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-clean-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Glo Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      For anyone looking up Gabbi Tuft's Glo protein: what it is called now, what the current label says, whether it is tested, what it costs per 25 g, and the Amazon alternatives worth considering.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#what-is-glo">Glo Is Now OURS</a>
      <a href="#ingredients">What Is in It</a>
      <a href="#collagen">The Collagen Question</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Third-Party Testing</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#buyer-feedback">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#vs">Glo vs Alternatives</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Glo protein powder is now sold as OURS Protein Powder: 25 g of protein per 36 g scoop, 120 to 140 calories, sweetened with allulose and monk fruit, at about $2.72 per 25 g. What changes the verdict is that collagen is the first ingredient and the brand lists no third-party testing. Below: the label, the GLO-to-OURS change, cost, buyer feedback and alternatives.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Glo Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Glo protein powder is the creator brand founded by Gabbi Tuft, and it is now sold as <strong>OURS Protein Powder</strong>: 25 g of protein and 120 to 140 calories per 36 g scoop, sweetened with allulose and monk fruit, at $67.99 for 25 servings, or about $2.72 per 25 g of protein. The old GLO product page now redirects to the OURS store, and the store still lists the seller as GLO by Gabbi.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our verdict: it is one of the cleanest-reading labels in the creator space, with no gums, no sucralose and no added sugar. But collagen is the first ingredient, there is no third-party certification, and it costs about 40% more per 25 g than a clean grass-fed whey. <strong>Buy it if</strong> you want a low-calorie, gum-free, allulose-sweetened shake and you trust the brand. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you want maximum muscle-building protein per dollar, need tested products, or loved the old GLO formula, which buyers say tasted and mixed differently.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Glo (OURS) by the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">25 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per 36 g scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">120-140</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories, by flavor</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Total and added sugar</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$2.72</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Cost per 25 g of protein</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Glo (OURS) Protein Powder and the Best Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            OURS is sold on Amazon by the brand itself, so the first two cards are the current Glo formula in its two most-reviewed flavors. The other three are the alternatives we would weigh against it: the closest creator-brand collagen blend, a certified whey isolate, and a cheaper clean whey. Cost per 25 g uses the label's servings and protein and the Amazon price we saw.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="what-is-glo" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is Glo Protein Powder, and Is It Now Called OURS?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes: Glo protein powder is now sold as OURS Protein Powder by GLO Ventures, Inc., the company Gabbi Tuft founded. The link that used to sell GLO's cold-processed whey on coachgabbi.com now forwards to oursrevolution.com, where the only protein powder for sale is OURS, and the Shopify store still names its vendor as "Glo by Gabbi". On Amazon, the OURS listings are sold by a seller called Glo by Gabbi too.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gabbi Tuft built her audience on TikTok and Instagram reviewing the ingredient labels of popular protein powders and bars, often calling out sucralose, acesulfame potassium, gums and seed oils. GLO was her answer to those videos. In her own posts at launch, she described GLO as an all-natural, non-GMO whey made in a GMP facility, third-party tested, sweetened only with stevia, with 24 g of protein per serving. Her posts also stress that she is the owner, not a paid partner, which is worth remembering when she reviews competing brands.
          </p>

          <p>
            OURS is not just a new name on the same bag. The current label differs from what Gabbi described for GLO in four ways: protein rose from 24 g to 25 g, stevia was replaced by allulose and monk fruit, hydrolyzed collagen peptides and organic coconut milk powder were added, and the formula now includes myo-inositol and a digestive enzyme blend. The brand calls the allulose and myo-inositol pairing a first for a protein powder. A Founder's Box FAQ on the brand's site confirms that the new formula carries allulose, myo-inositol and collagen.
          </p>

          <p>
            That matters for anyone searching "glo protein powder" because the reviews, recipes and ingredient breakdowns you find online may describe the old stevia whey, not the product you can buy today. Everything below is checked against the current OURS nutrition facts panels on the brand's site. Where we refer to the original GLO, we say so and cite Gabbi's own description, since its old product page is no longer live.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Original GLO (per Gabbi's posts)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">OURS today (per label)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein per serving</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein sources</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Collagen, milk protein isolate, whey concentrate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweetener</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stevia only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Allulose and monk fruit</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Added extras</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None stated</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Myo-inositol, coconut milk powder, enzymes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Testing claim</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">"3rd party tested"</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No testing claim on the product page</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="ingredients" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in Glo (OURS) Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every OURS flavor gives 25 g of protein in a 36 g scoop with 0 g of total sugars and 0 g of added sugars, per the brand's nutrition facts panels at the time of writing. Calories run 120 for Salted Caramel, 130 for Chocolate and 140 for Vanilla. Each pouch holds 25 servings, and net weights are printed as 32 oz (894 to 907 g depending on flavor).
          </p>

          <p>
            The ingredient list is short and recognisable. Salted Caramel reads: hydrolyzed collagen peptides, milk protein isolate, allulose, whey protein concentrate, organic coconut milk powder on tapioca dextrin, natural flavor, pink Himalayan salt, myo-inositol, monk fruit extract and a digestive enzyme blend (amylase, protease, lactase, lipase, beta-glucanase, papain, phytase). Chocolate adds alkalized cocoa powder. Vanilla moves the coconut milk powder up to second place. All three declare milk as the only major allergen.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
