            What is missing matters as much as what is there. There is no sucralose, acesulfame potassium, xanthan gum, guar gum, carrageenan, lecithin or added oil on any of the three labels, and the brand lists "no seed oils, no artificial flavors, no artificial sweeteners, no carrageenan" on the product page. That is a genuinely short label by protein-powder standards, and it is why the brand appeals to readers of our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">best clean protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What do allulose and myo-inositol actually do?</h3>

          <p>
            Allulose is a rare sugar that tastes like sugar but is barely metabolised; the FDA lets brands leave it out of total and added sugars on the label, which is why OURS can print 0 g of sugar while listing allulose third. It counts in total carbohydrate, so it is likely part of the 3 to 5 g shown. In large amounts it can cause bloating or loose stools in some people. Myo-inositol has been studied mainly in women with PCOS, at doses around 2 to 4 g a day. OURS does not print how much allulose or myo-inositol each scoop contains, so the brand's claims that the pair helps curb hunger and reduce fat storage cannot be checked against those studies. If myo-inositol is your reason for buying, see our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-pcos/">protein powder for PCOS</a> guide and talk to your doctor.
          </p>

          <p>
            The brand also says every flavor meets a "10x protein rule" of at least 10 grams of protein for every calorie. Read literally, that cannot be right, since 25 g of protein sits alongside 120 to 140 calories. The useful figures are the label's own: about 18 to 21 g of protein per 100 calories, which is lean but not exceptional for a dairy protein.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="collagen" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Much of the 25 g Is Collagen, and Does It Matter?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Collagen is the first ingredient in every OURS flavor, which under US labeling rules means it is the largest single ingredient by weight. The brand does not say how many of the 25 g of protein come from collagen versus milk protein isolate and whey, and it does not publish an amino acid profile, so the exact split cannot be calculated from the label.
          </p>

          <p>
            It matters because collagen is not a complete protein. It contains no tryptophan and is low in leucine, the amino acid most closely tied to switching on muscle protein synthesis. A scoop that is heavy in collagen will still count fully toward the protein number on the label, but it will do less for muscle building than 25 g of whey would. Our <a href="/collagen-vs-protein-powder/">collagen vs protein powder</a> breakdown covers the difference in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            That does not make OURS a bad product. Milk protein isolate and whey concentrate are both complete, high-leucine proteins, and they make up a meaningful part of the scoop. For a shake you drink mainly for fullness, convenience and daily protein intake, the blend is fine. It is a weaker choice if you are lifting to build muscle, are an older adult trying to protect muscle mass, or are counting on each shake to deliver a full dose of leucine.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also a labelling wrinkle on Amazon. The OURS listing titles describe a "cold-processed whey blend, whey isolate", but the printed ingredient lists name milk protein isolate and whey concentrate, not whey isolate, and put collagen ahead of both. Milk protein isolate contains casein as well as whey. Trust the printed label over the listing title, and if you have questions, ask the brand for the collagen share before you buy.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Read the first ingredient, not the front of the bag</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">When collagen leads the list, the protein number overstates how much muscle-building protein you get. For lifting goals, a whey or milk protein that lists no collagen is the better tool.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is Glo Protein Powder Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No certification we could find: OURS is not listed with NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, and the current product page makes no third-party testing claim. When GLO launched, Gabbi described it in her own posts as "3rd party tested" and made in a GMP facility, but the brand has not published a certificate of analysis, a lab name or results for either the old or the new formula on its product page.
          </p>

          <p>
            The OURS product page does carry a California Proposition 65 warning that the product can expose you to chemicals including lead. Prop 65 warnings are common on cocoa, collagen and plant-based powders, and they are triggered by a low threshold, so the warning alone does not mean the product is unsafe. It does mean the brand chose to warn rather than publish a test result showing it falls below the threshold. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> guide explains how to read these warnings.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most buyers, this is the biggest gap in the Glo story. A brand built on telling people what is wrong with other labels would earn more trust by posting batch test results for lead, cadmium and arsenic and by putting the product through a certification such as Informed Choice. Until it does, the honest answer to "is Glo protein third-party tested" is that the brand does not say so for the current product.
          </p>

          <p>
            If testing is non-negotiable, for example because you compete in a tested sport or you are pregnant, pick a product with a public certificate. Transparent Labs lists Informed Choice certification, and our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">NSF certified protein powder</a> guide lists more. Anyone who is pregnant, has kidney disease or takes medication should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding myo-inositol or any new supplement.
          </p>
        </div>

