        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Glo Protein Powder, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Glo, now OURS, suits people who want a short, gum-free label, no sucralose or stevia, 0 g of sugar and a low-calorie shake, and who are happy to pay a premium for it. It makes most sense as a fullness-focused snack shake, a coffee add-in or a yogurt mix-in, which is how many of its buyers describe using it. It is also a reasonable pick for women already following Gabbi's content who want myo-inositol in a daily habit, provided they check the dose question with their doctor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if your main goal is building or preserving muscle. With collagen leading the ingredient list, each scoop gives less leucine than 25 g of whey, and a plain whey or whey isolate delivers more complete protein for less money. Skip it, too, if you need a certified product, if you are sensitive to coconut, or if you are prone to digestive upset from sugar alcohols and rare sugars like allulose. Older adults with medical conditions, anyone who is pregnant or breastfeeding, and people with kidney disease should ask a doctor or registered dietitian first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Former GLO buyers are their own group. If you bought GLO for the stevia-sweetened whey taste, the reviews strongly suggest OURS is a different product. A plain stevia-sweetened whey such as Transparent Labs is closer to what GLO was, at a lower price per gram and with a public certification.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a broader shortlist with the same priorities, start with our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">best clean protein powder</a> rankings, then cross-check against <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-women/">protein powder for women</a> and <a href="/best-collagen-protein-powder/">collagen protein powder</a> if collagen is something you specifically want.
          </p>
