        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming OURS is the same product as GLO</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The name change came with a new formula. Stevia was replaced by allulose and monk fruit, and collagen, coconut milk powder, myo-inositol and enzymes were added. Reviews and ingredient breakdowns written about GLO in 2025 describe a different product from the one you can buy today, so check the current label before ordering.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting all 25 g as muscle-building protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Collagen is the first ingredient, and collagen has no tryptophan and little leucine. The 25 g on the label is accurate, but a collagen-led scoop does less for muscle protein synthesis than 25 g of whey. If you lift, count it as partial protein or pair it with a complete source.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading "0 g sugar" as "no sweetener"</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                OURS prints 0 g of total and added sugar because the FDA lets brands leave allulose out of those lines. Allulose is still the third ingredient in Salted Caramel and it still counts in carbohydrates. Some people get bloating or loose stools from larger amounts, so start with one scoop a day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking a creator's word for testing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gabbi described GLO as third-party tested, but the current product page shows no certification, no lab name and no results, and it carries a Prop 65 lead warning. If testing matters to you, ask the brand for a certificate of analysis, or buy a product listed with Informed Choice or NSF.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Subscribing before trying a flavor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Buyer reviews are sharply split on taste and mixing, especially among former GLO customers who report coconut notes, clumping and aftertaste. At $67.99 a bag, buy one pouch of the flavor reviewers rate best, usually Chocolate, before committing to a four-weekly subscription you then have to cancel.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
